{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Of Zerah</b> — he is Zohar (Genesis 46:10), in the sense of צֹהַר (window). But the family of Ohad ceased to exist. And similarly, five families from the tribe of Benjamin are missing, for he had ten sons when he went down to Egypt (ibid. v. 21), yet here he counts only five — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 26:13). He further wrote (Rashi on Numbers 26:24): all the families were named after those who went down to Egypt, and those born from there onward were not called families, except for the families of Ephraim and Manasseh, all of whom were born in Egypt, and Ard and Naaman, sons of Bela son of Benjamin. And I found in the foundation of Rabbi Moses the Darshan that their mother went down to Egypt while pregnant with them. Now if this is an aggadah, well and good; but if not, I would say that Bela had many sons, and from these two — Ard and Naaman — there issued from each one a great family, and the descendants of the other sons were called by the name of Bela, while the descendants of these two were called by their own names. All this is the language of the Rav, of blessed memory. But I marvel at him, for the difficulty is not because Scripture counts the family of the Belaite by itself and the family of the Ardite and the family of the Naamite — his sons — by themselves, since this would be because they had become families, according to the Rav's words, and so Scripture does likewise with the sons of Judah, the sons of Manasseh and Ephraim, and likewise with the sons of Asher. Rather, if we say that Ard and Naaman were born to Bela son of Benjamin after his descent to Egypt, they would not be fit to be counted here as families! And if we suppose they were born to him beforehand, there would be more than seventy souls, for then the sons of Benjamin would be ten and these are two sons of Bela! Therefore Rabbi Moses the Darshan interpreted that their mother was pregnant with them, and he does not count them there, but here he reckons them as having been born. And if this is an aggadah, we shall accept the difficulty for the sake of receiving it, and we would still need to say that Jochebed was born between the walls on the day they entered there, and that is why he mentions her among the seventy souls, while these were born after months. But if it is not a tradition from our Sages, I reject this reasoning with both hands. Rather, we can say that Ard and Naaman, sons of Benjamin, died without sons, and Bela wished to establish a name for his brothers in Israel, so he called his sons by the name of his dead brothers. Or perhaps he performed levirate marriage with their wives, for he was the firstborn, and the Ard and Naaman who were sons of Bela became heads of families to establish a name for Ard and Naaman, sons of Benjamin, who went down to Egypt. But what seems correct in my eyes is that Naaman and Ard, whom he counts among the sons of Benjamin there in the portion of 'And these are the names' (Genesis 46:21), are the sons of his firstborn son Bela, as is explicitly stated here, and so he counts them in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 8:1–5). And it is the way of Scripture to make the sons of the sons as though they were sons, as when it says 'Laban the son of Nahor' (Genesis 29:5), and it is written in Chronicles 'the sons of Shem: Elam and Asshur and Arphaxad and Lud and Aram and Uz and Hul and Gether and Mash' (1 Chronicles 1:17) — and behold, these last four are his grandsons! And although he did not do likewise with the sons of Perez and the sons of Beriah, perhaps they were born on the knees of Benjamin and he reckoned them to himself in the manner of 'And these are the generations of Aaron and Moses' (Numbers 3:1), or because the sons of Benjamin were many, being eight, he attached the minority to the majority. And by way of reasoning it is further possible for us to say that Scripture did not count the families only for those who went down to Egypt, for also those born in Egypt from that day onward it counted as families, as it did with Ephraim and Manasseh. And there is no reason to do so because they were in Egypt, for at the time of the descent it is fitting that all of them be counted as one number and families be made from the seventy souls, and so Scripture counts them everywhere among those who went down to Egypt: 'With seventy souls your fathers went down to Egypt' (Deuteronomy 10:22). And the sons of Bela were born afterward. Rather, the matter is that it was the custom in Israel to appoint for themselves heads of father's houses; all those who issued from a man's loins would trace their lineage forever to that man and would be called by his name, for his honor, just as today all the Ishmaelites and all Israel dwelling in their lands do, to be called all of them by families — Ibn Ezra, Ibn Shoshan. And this is what is said, 'These are the heads of their fathers' houses' (Exodus 6:14), for from the time they were fruitful and multiplied in Egypt, they established for themselves heads of families to trace their lineage after them. And perhaps this matter began in Egypt so that they would not intermingle with the nations and would be distinct and known by their tribes — 'There the tribes went up, the tribes of Jah, a testimony for Israel' — and it became a statute in Israel. And so those mentioned here — each man as head of his father's house — the family would trace itself to him. Therefore he counts in the house of Machir the family of the Machirite and the family of the Gileadite, his son, and the family of the Iezerite and the Helekite, his sons and their brothers, and likewise with the sons of Judah and Ephraim, for these sons mentioned here were great and honored and became heads. This was not because they begot a great family, according to the Rav's words, for all of them begot many families, for they were fruitful and swarmed and multiplied and grew exceedingly mighty; but rather it was for the honor of those appointed over them as heads. And most of the families, while they were in Egypt, traced their lineage to those who went down to Egypt, for they were the honored fathers; while the rest made for themselves, from those born there shortly before, heads of father's houses. And therefore most of those mentioned here were those who went down to Egypt, and behold the lineage of these families traces to those who went down to Egypt, for there they established them."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The quote 'שם עלו שבטים שבטי יה עדות לישראל' is from Psalms 122:4; Ramban cites it fragmentarily without a full verse address. The phrase 'הרב ז״ל' in context refers to Maimonides, who is cited by name earlier as 'ר׳ משה הדרשן' is a different figure."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:16.550369370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}